course: dictionary
lesson: 9046
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sledge is a small vehicle with long, narrow runners instead of wheels. It slides over snow or ice, often down a hill for fun. «Workers dragged stone blocks on sledges over sand wetted to make it slippery.» The sentence tells you what a sledge does: it carries heavy things by sliding.
explain: Sledge can also be a verb, a doing word. To sledge means to ride a sledge. «A sledge at the top of a hill whizzes down with no push.» Here sledge is a thing, a noun, but the same word can name the ride too: children go sledging on winter hills.
explain: Some sentences give clues about a word's meaning. «Stonehenge is a great circle of huge stones raised by farming people using ropes, sledges and wooden rollers.» Ropes and rollers are used for moving heavy things, so a sledge must be something that carries heavy loads too.
explain: Context also tells you how a sledge behaves. «A sledge slides far on smooth ice but stops quickly on grass or gravel.» The words slides and stops show that the surface changes how far it goes. Check by swapping in your meaning: a sledge is a sliding vehicle, and that still makes sense.
fact: A sledge has long, narrow runners instead of wheels.
fact: «Sledge» can be a noun (a sledge, sledges) or a verb (to sledge, sledging, sledged).
fact: A large sledge can be pulled by dogs, horses or reindeer.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, sledging in cricket means saying rude things to put a player off.
fact: Safety rules for sledging say go down feet first, check the bottom of the hill is clear, and wear a helmet.
exercise: What is a sledge? | A tall tree with thick roots | A warm boot for walking in snow | *A small vehicle with long, narrow runners, used for sliding over snow or ice | A big bag for carrying shopping
exercise: «Rough surfaces make more friction than smooth ones: a sledge slides far on smooth ice but stops quickly on grass or gravel.» Where does a sledge slide furthest? | On a rough path | On grass | On gravel | *On smooth ice
exercise: What does «to sledge» mean? | To pull a cart with a horse | *To ride a sledge | To cook over an open fire | To swim in a cold lake
test: «Go sledging» means what? | *Riding a sledge for fun, often down a hill | Reading a map of the mountains | Swimming under the ice | Building a snowman in the garden
test: «Workers dragged stone blocks on sledges over sand wetted to make it slippery.» Why was the sand wetted? | To cool it down | To wash the stones clean | To make it dry and hard | *To make it slippery
exam: «Sledges are hard to stop on packed snow and ice, so a slope that runs out onto water or a road is a trap.» Why is such a slope a trap? | Because the slope is too smooth to walk on | Because sledges float on water | *Because a sledge is hard to stop on snow and ice, so it can run onto water or a road | Because roads are made of snow
